{
  "moduleId": "m00304",
  "title": {
    "segmentId": "m00304:title:auto-1",
    "text": "Reactions of Monosaccharides"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "e8189646-87f1-4918-bba5-5c476dc82d05"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00001",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00304:title:sect-00001-title",
        "text": "Ester and Ether Formation"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00002",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00002"
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00003",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00003"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00001",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00304:alt:fig-00001-alt",
              "text": "Beta-D-Glucopyranose reacts with acetic anhydride in pyridine at zero degree Celsius to form penta-O-acetyl-beta-D-glucopyranose (91 percent yield)."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_25_06_001.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00004",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00004"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00002",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00304:alt:fig-00002-alt",
              "text": "Alpha-D-Glucopyranose reacts with iodomethane in the presence of silver oxide to form alpha-D-glucopyranose pentamethyl ether (85 percent yield)."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_25_06_002.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00002",
      "class": "section-exercises",
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00304:title:sect-00002-title",
        "text": "Problems"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "exercise",
          "id": "exer-00001",
          "problem": {
            "content": []
          },
          "solution": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00003",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00304:title:sect-00003-title",
        "text": "Glycoside Formation"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00006",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00006"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00003",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00304:alt:fig-00003-alt",
              "text": "The reversible reaction of a hemiacetal with alcohol in the presence of an acid catalyst yields an acetal in which anomeric O H has been replaced by O R group."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_25_06_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00007",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00007"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00004",
          "class": "unnumbered",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00304:alt:fig-00004-alt",
              "text": "The reversible reaction of beta-D-glucopyranose with methanol in the presence of an acid gives a mixture of alpha and beta methyl D-glucopyranosides in sixty-six and thirty-three percent yield, respectively."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_25_06_005.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00008",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00008"
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00009",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00009"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00005",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00304:alt:fig-00005-alt",
              "text": "The structure of digitoxin, a glycoside. The trisaccharide and steroid in the structure are labeled."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_25_06_006.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00010",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00010"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00006",
          "class": "unnumbered",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00304:alt:fig-00006-alt",
              "text": "Treatment of pentaacetyl-beta-D-glucopyranose with hydrogen bromide converts anomeric O H to bromine and forms tetraacetyl-alpha-D-pyranosyl bromide. It undergoes further reaction and forms methylarbutin."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_25_06_007.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00011",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00011"
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00012",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00012"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00007",
          "class": "scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00304:caption:fig-00007-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00304:alt:fig-00007-alt",
              "text": "Tetraacetyl-D-glucopyranosyl bromide undergoes the elimination of bromide and the anomeric cation is stabilized by neighboring C 2 acetyl. Alcohol displacement of the oxonium ion gives a beta-glycoside."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_25_06_008.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00013",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00013"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00004",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00304:title:sect-00004-title",
        "text": "Biological Ester Formation: Phosphorylation"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00014",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00014"
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00015",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00015"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00008",
          "class": "scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00304:caption:fig-00008-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00304:alt:fig-00008-alt",
              "text": "The conversion of D-glucose to a glycoprotein through three different steps is depicted. All the structures are in the pyranose form (Haworth projection)."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_25_06_009.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00005",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00304:title:sect-00005-title",
        "text": "Reduction of Monosaccharides"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00016",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00016"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00009",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00304:alt:fig-00009-alt",
              "text": "The chair structure of beta-D-glucopyranose is converted to open chain, shown as a Fischer projection. This undergoes reduction with sodium borohydride and water to form D-glucitol which is an alditol."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_25_06_010.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00017",
          "segmentId": "m00304:para:para-00017"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00006",
      "class": "section-exercises",
